<worksheet>
  <title>Homework 09</title>

  <objectives>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            The purpose of the homework is to:
        </p>
    </introduction>
   <ul>
        <li>
            <p>
             Practice applying the concepts and techniques discussed this week
            </p>
        </li>

        <li>
            <p>
             Reveal gaps in your understanding so you know what to study
            </p>
        </li>

        <li>
            <p>
                Enable a productive re-read of the textbook chapter/engagement with
                other course resources.
            </p>
        </li>
    </ul>
    <conclusion>
        <p>
          The homework is both a valuable learning opportunity and a gauge of how
          well you're doing. Did you solve all the problems by yourself with no help? Incredible!
          If that didn't happen, that's OK: we're not expected to get everything right the first time.
          What you need to do is look carefully at what you needed help with, and then make a study plan,
          which will likely include solving more problems, to fill in the gaps in your understanding.
        </p>
    </conclusion>
  </objectives>
  
  <introduction>
    <p>
      <alert>Instructions:</alert> Complete all the exercises below and submit your work on Canvas by the due date.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <page>

    <exercise>
      <title>Properties of Angular Momentum and Moment of Inertia</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        7.1 Hint: this problem is easier if you use vector notation and, yes, you can
                        <q>take out</q> a constant vector out of a sum if you do it carefully.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        7.7 Hint: use the parallel axis theorem to find the moment of inertia of a horizontal strip
                        of width dy. You can do this and 7.8 after everything else.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        7.8 Hint: a sphere = many thin disks rotating about the axis.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <title>Statics</title>
        <p>
            7.5 Hint: a good picture goes a long way. Note that you will find
            3 equations for 4 unknowns, but you can solve for the sum of the
            friction forces, which will be enough here.
        </p>        
    </exercise>


    <exercise>
        <title>Using the angular momentum principle</title>
            <p>
                7.10 Note that the CM of the cylinder is at rest, so the
                forces on it should balance.
            </p>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <title>Putting Everything Together</title>
        <p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        7.3 Careful! The speed <m>v</m> is
                        <alert>relative to the ring</alert>. Remember to
                        use the parallel axis theorem.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        7.4
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        7.13
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        7.20 Remember that the acceleration of the CM has two
                        components: one parallel to the velocity, and one perpendicular. This
                        is probably the hardest problem in this HW, as it will require using
                        forces, angular momentum, and energy. But it's quite rewarding, your
                        final answer should be simple!
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        7.33
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </p>
        
    </exercise>
  </page>
</worksheet>